# The Tower of Hanoi: why 2ⁿ − 1 moves
subtitle: "Why moving a tower of n disks takes exactly 2ⁿ − 1 moves: the doubling at the heart of recursion, then solve the puzzle yourself."

## The Tower of Hanoi: why 2ⁿ − 1 moves
use: tower_of_hanoi
with: {"disks":3,"box":{"x":-200,"y":70,"w":1400,"h":770}}

    math rec tex "M_n = {2M_{n-1}} + 1" size 56 x 500 y 615 hidden true
    math seq tex "M_n = 2^n - 1" size 56 x 500 y 545 hidden true
    portrait portrait_edouard_lucas x 880 y 600 width 120 of "Édouard Lucas" cameo true hidden true
    text tally_1 "3 moves" x 500 y 490 font_size 44 hidden true
    text tally_2 "3 + 1" x 500 y 490 font_size 44 hidden true
    text tally_3 "3 + 1 + 3 = 7" x 500 y 490 font_size 44 hidden true
    text counts "1 · 3 · 7 · 15 · 31" #2f6b8f x 500 y 490 font_size 44 hidden true
    text years "64 disks: 585 billion years" #b5482e x 420 y 490 font_size 40 hidden true

    hide title

    card title "The Tower of Hanoi: why 2ⁿ − 1 moves"

The Tower of Hanoi asks how few moves shift a whole tower to the right peg. The rules: one disk at a time, never a larger on a smaller.
    draw peg_0 peg_1 peg_2 disk_3 disk_2 disk_1

Take three disks. Before the biggest can move anywhere, what has to be true?
    highlight disk_3

Both disks above it must be out of the way, stacked on the middle peg. That is a two-disk tower, moved: three moves.
    draw tally_1

    move disk_1 disk_2 by [326.7,-28] duration 1.2

    erase tally_1

Then the big disk crosses, in one move.
    draw tally_2

    move disk_3 by [653.4,0] duration 1

    erase tally_2

And the two-disk tower moves again, onto it: three more. Three, one, three: seven.
    draw tally_3

    move disk_1 disk_2 by [326.7,28] duration 1.2

    erase tally_3

The same holds for any size: to move n disks, move the tower above the biggest twice, plus one move for the biggest.
    draw rec

That twice is the whole story. Each extra disk doubles the work, and adds one.
    highlight rec part "{2M_{n-1}}"

One, three, seven, fifteen, thirty-one: always one less than a power of two. A guess of two more per disk would say nine for four disks; it takes fifteen.
    draw seq counts

    erase counts

Édouard Lucas sold the puzzle in 1883 with a legend of monks moving sixty-four disks. At a move a second, that takes about 585 billion years.
    draw portrait_edouard_lucas years

    erase portrait_edouard_lucas years

Back to the start. Now it's your tower.
    move disk_1 disk_2 disk_3 by [-653.4,0] duration 1

    draw counter

    ask "Move the tower to the right peg. Can you do it in seven?" widget tower_of_hanoi answer solved
        right: Solved. Seven is the fewest: the small pair aside in three, the big disk over, the pair back on top in three.
        wrong: Get the top two onto the middle peg first; that frees the big disk.

    quiz "Five disks take 31 moves. How many does six take?"
        right: 63: the five-disk tower moves twice, 31 moves each, plus one move for the biggest disk.
        * 33
        * 36
        + 63
